The Common Foundation of Slavery:
Institutional Slavery and Mental Enslavement in the Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass and Goethe’s Faust


        Slavery exists both as an institution and within the mind. The institution of slavery, as seen in the Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, functions under external means, whether legally through written law, illegally through the use of force, drugs, and/or coercion, or through both legal and illegal practice. Slavery of the mind seldom appears obviously, and operates internally, with no surety of detectable symptoms or outward expression. For example, the domestication of elephants uses principles of mental slavery by tying young elephants to a tree with a thick rope. When young, the elephant learns that he lacks strength to break the rope, and gives up. When old, even a string holds him. A sensible elephant acquires learned helplessness after making great effort to break his bonds without the power to do so and remains unaware throughout his life of any alternate to his captivity the rope imposes. In the same manner, a man, Faust from Goethe’s Faust, for example, enslaves himself to habits, desires, and conventions, all the while unaware of any alternative to an unchanging state of imprisonment within his own mind.
        The institution of slavery requires mental slavery in order to function. However, degrees of freedom within the mind must be considered, as well. Total freedom, for ease of reference, enlightenment, theoretically can exist anywhere but in the realm of practicality, lesser degrees of freedom play the more important role in regards to institutionalized and internal slavery. Those enslaved must lack awareness of the transience of their state in order to remain enslaved. At a young age, just as the elephant, Frederick Douglass witnesses the trademarks of slavery, whipping, slaves treated as property, and punishments given to slaves who in any way defy their place in society. Frederick Douglass consequently grows up believing the sole alternative to his life as a slave rests in death. However, he realizes the possibility of another escape when he meets Mrs. Auld. Frederick Douglass recounts his time with Mrs. Auld in Baltimore, remarking, “She did not seem to perceive that I sustained to her the relation of a mere chattel, and that for her to treat me as a human being was not only wrong, but dangerously so.” Frederick Douglass, up to that point only handled as property, begins to understand his humanity and its impossibility of coexistence with his status as a slave.
        Internal slavery, while not as obvious as external slavery, prevails much more widely and presents myriad more dangers to the happiness of the common man or woman than do the dangers and horrors of institutional slavery. Faust, ironically stating, “how hard I’ve slaved away,” in his first four lines of speech, remains unaware of his inability to break from his constant cravings. While God in Goethe’s Faust views staunch striving as a virtue, Faust’s enslavement to his obsession of finding unknown knowledge erect the bars of the cell that keep him from happiness.
        The primal danger and ruin of mental slavery manifests itself in its subtlety. Disregarding the Greek maxim “know thyself”, Faust devotes his life to pursuing what he believes he desires. Faust labors for naught because he knows not what he truly wants, and thus never fulfills his deepest desire. Fortunately, his pursuits rival those most noble and he, aspiring to an unattainable perfection, becomes more noble in his yearnings, but fails to find rest due to the perpetual oppression of his own mind. In such a way, akin to the Apostle Paul’s profession of servitude or slavery to Christ, inward slavery to noble aspirations may produce noble men, but it prevents them from finding peace.
        Goethe’s perspective of God shows Him pleased not with perfection, but rather with struggle, the very enslavement in which Faust finds himself. Faust, however, finds no pleasure in his virtuous struggle. To break Faust from his unfulfilling intellectual pursuits, Mephistopheles wagers Faust with the intention of making him his slave under institutional slavery. Mephistopheles detours Faust from his quest for nobility in knowledge to seeking the great unknown in human experience and worldly pleasure. As Faust cannot satisfy his desire, the continual and novel supply of consumption on Mephistopheles part to Faust inhibits Faust’s ability to understand his continued and progressively worsening inward slavery. Mephistopheles, in offering Faust things continually new, continually different, and all equally ephemeral and transient, feeds Faust’s insatiable appetite for meaning in his life. 
        It remains entirely possible, however, that to break from his enslavement, Faust must become totally aware of the depravity of his pursuits and the effects it has on others. Faust comes close to realizing the reality of his situation when he berates Mephistopheles in prose concerning Margarete, saying, “Delivered up to evil spirits and the stony-hearted justice of mankind! And meanwhile you distract me with your insipid entertainments, keep her situation, more desperate every day, from me, and leave her to perish helplessly!” Interpreted as metaphor, the entirety of Faust’s pursuits pale in comparison to more worthy, simple, and obvious callings.
        The ability to change lies solely in the perception. Frederick Douglass, if he did not know of the humanity of slaves and that they were not, after all, doomed to lifelong slavery, would never find freedom for himself in society, let alone make an effort to abolish slavery or decry its foundations. In the same way, Faust, through his experiences, gains new perspective not only to the assurance of failure in his pursuits of perfection but to the acceptance of not gaining what he has for so long sought. When the mind becomes aware of the falsity of stasis, indeed, in the inevitability of change, it breaks free from its current bonds of slavery, giving the institutionalized slave the reasoning and motivation to fight against the injustices of society and the mentally enslaved the understanding to break their impractical, unfruitful, and damaging habits.